 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Shark NZ801UK anti hair wrap upright - suction has gone really weak over the last couple of weeks, barely picks up crumbs. Have emptied the bin. Any ideas before I buy a new one?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Switch it off and unplug it (or take the battery off) first. You do not need to buy a new one yet; weak suction is almost always fixable. The most common cause after emptying the bin is that the filters are clogged or still damp. Please wash both the pre-motor and HEPA filters in cold water only and leave them to dry fully for at least 24 hours before reinserting them. While they are drying, detach the hose and wand and look through each one to check for any blockages (like a coin or large debris) that might be restricting airflow. If the filters were already clean and dry, please let me know what you find when looking through the hose.\n[Helpful to see: image \"Poor suction — what to check first\" — Lost suction is usually a full bin, a dirty filter or a blockage in the hose, tube or floor head — check and clear those before suspecting the motor. Switch off and unplug first. Typical layout — your model may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Coin goes through the hose fine and the wand's clear, I can see light through it. The filters have never been washed, I didn't know they were there. No burning smell or cut-outs, just a dusty smell from the exhaust. I'll wash them now and leave them to dry."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That suggests the airflow path (hose and wand) is clear, so the weak suction is almost probably caused by the filters being choked with fine dust. Since they have never been washed, they are likely acting like a solid block of felt. The dusty smell you are getting from the exhaust is typical when air is forced through a saturated filter. 1. Remove both the pre-motor and HEPA filters (usually located near where the bin slides out).\n2. Do not use soap or put them in the washing machine.\n3. Shake off excess water and leave them to air dry completely for at least 24 hours in a warm, well-ventilated area. They must be 100% bone-dry before you put them back in, otherwise they will still block airflow and can damage the motor. Once they are fully dry, reassemble the vacuum and test it on hard floor first (to avoid sucking up dust if there's any residual moisture). If suction is restored, you should wash these filters every 3 months to prevent this from happening again. Let me know how it performs once they are dry."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Washed both and left them 24 hours till bone dry. Massive difference, suction is back to like new. Thanks."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That is excellent news! It was most likely the clogged filters that were choking the airflow. To keep it running like new for longer, try washing those filters every 3 months or whenever you notice the suction starting to drop again. If anything else comes up with your Shark, just let me know."
  }
 ],
